// Facade: process parse_aws_guardduty,
//         process aws_guardduty_to_otlp
// Category: Cloud security findings
// Test corpus: spec-only (AWS documentation per-finding-type sample bodies;
//              cross-referenced against the CreateSampleFindings API shape —
//              verify against live findings before production rollout)
//
// AWS GuardDuty finding JSON parser
//
// Vendor notes — Amazon Web Services GuardDuty — the AWS-native threat
//           detection service. Findings synthesise signals from
//           CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, DNS query logs, EKS audit
//           logs, S3 data events, malware-protection scans and the
//           managed threat-intel feed; each finding is a single
//           detection event keyed by `id` within an `accountId` /
//           `region` / `detectorId` scope.
// Wire —     JSON — one GuardDuty finding object per `ingress`.
//           Top-level fields — `schemaVersion`, `accountId`, `region`,
//           `partition`, `id`, `arn`, `type`, `severity` (Float
//           1.0–10.0), `title`, `description`, `createdAt`,
//           `updatedAt`, plus the nested `resource.*` and
//           `service.*` payloads. The wire is identical across the
//           three common delivery paths (EventBridge default bus,
//           SNS topic from the GuardDuty console, direct findings
//           export to S3 / EventBridge); transports either pass the
//           bare finding or wrap it under a `detail` envelope which
//           the upstream bridge unwraps before invoking this parser.
//           Reference —
//           https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_findings.html
//
// Sample input (Recon:EC2/Portscan, anonymised to RFC 5737):
//   {"schemaVersion":"2.0","accountId":"123456789012","region":"us-east-1","partition":"aws","id":"abcd1234efgh5678ijkl","arn":"arn:aws:guardduty:us-east-1:123456789012:detector/12abc34d567e8fa901bc2d34e56789f0/finding/abcd1234efgh5678ijkl","type":"Recon:EC2/Portscan","resource":{"resourceType":"Instance","instanceDetails":{"instanceId":"i-99999999","instanceType":"t2.micro","availabilityZone":"us-east-1a"}},"service":{"action":{"actionType":"PORT_PROBE","portProbeAction":{"portProbeDetails":[{"localPortDetails":{"port":22,"portName":"SSH"},"remoteIpDetails":{"ipAddressV4":"198.51.100.10","country":{"countryName":"United States"}}}]}},"archived":false,"count":3,"detectorId":"12abc34d567e8fa901bc2d34e56789f0","eventFirstSeen":"2026-05-20T03:14:15Z","eventLastSeen":"2026-05-20T04:00:00Z"},"severity":2.0,"title":"Unprotected port on EC2 instance i-99999999 is being probed.","description":"EC2 instance has an unprotected port which is being probed by a known malicious host.","createdAt":"2026-05-20T03:14:16Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-20T04:00:01Z"}
//
// Severity mapping (`severity` is a required Double on the AWS wire;
// ranges follow "Severity levels for GuardDuty findings"):
//   1.0 – 3.9   → OTel 13 WARN
//   4.0 – 6.9   → OTel 17 ERROR
//   7.0 – 8.9   → OTel 19 ERROR3
//   9.0 – 10.0  → OTel 21 FATAL
//
// `finding_info.types[]` derivation: GuardDuty's `type` field
// follows the closed shape `<ThreatPurpose>:<ResourceType>/<ThreatName>`
// (sometimes with a `.<Variant>` suffix on ThreatName, e.g.
// `UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/ConsoleLoginSuccess.B`). We
// regex-split into three OCSF type tokens; the variant suffix
// stays inside the threat-name token to preserve sub-detector
// distinction.
//
// Direct call — this snippet handles a single OCSF class (2004); the
// dispatch is purely for validating the category vocabulary. Call
// as `process parse_aws_guardduty` directly.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helpers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// GuardDuty numeric severity → OTel SeverityNumber. Invalid values
// return null; the parser boundary distinguishes them from absent
// severity and reports malformed findings before dispatch.
def function aws_guardduty_severity_number(severity) {
    switch true {
        severity == null                       { null }
        severity >= 1.0 and severity < 4.0    { 13 }
        severity >= 4.0 and severity < 7.0    { 17 }
        severity >= 7.0 and severity < 9.0    { 19 }
        severity >= 9.0 and severity <= 10.0  { 21 }
        default                                { null }
    }
}

// Split GuardDuty `type` into its three OCSF type tokens.
//   "Recon:EC2/Portscan" →
//     { category: "Recon", resource_type: "EC2", threat_name: "Portscan" }
// Returns null when the wire does not match the closed shape — the
// caller routes that to error_log.
def function aws_guardduty_split_type(type_str) {
    regex_parse(
        type_str,
        "^(?P<category>[A-Za-z]+):(?P<resource_type>[A-Za-z0-9]+)/(?P<threat_name>[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)$"
    )
}

// Build the OCSF `resources[]` entry from `resource.*`. Each
// GuardDuty `resourceType` selects a different sibling object on
// the wire (`instanceDetails` for Instance, `accessKeyDetails` for
// AccessKey, `s3BucketDetails` for S3Bucket, etc.); we surface the
// raw vendor block under `unmapped` so the composer can still emit
// it without the parser having to teach the composer every AWS
// resource shape. The minimal OCSF Resource fields (`type`, `uid`,
// `name`) are populated from the most-distinctive identifier
// available per resourceType.
def function aws_guardduty_resource(resource) {
    switch true {
        resource == null { null }
        resource.resourceType == "Instance" {
            {
                type: "Instance",
                uid:  resource.instanceDetails.instanceId,
                name: resource.instanceDetails.instanceId,
                cloud_partition: null,
                region:          resource.instanceDetails.availabilityZone,
                unmapped:        resource.instanceDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "AccessKey" {
            {
                type: "AccessKey",
                uid:  resource.accessKeyDetails.accessKeyId,
                name: resource.accessKeyDetails.userName,
                unmapped: resource.accessKeyDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "S3Bucket" {
            {
                type: "S3Bucket",
                uid:  resource.s3BucketDetails.arn,
                name: resource.s3BucketDetails.name,
                unmapped: resource.s3BucketDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "EKSCluster" {
            {
                type: "EKSCluster",
                uid:  resource.eksClusterDetails.arn,
                name: resource.eksClusterDetails.name,
                unmapped: resource.eksClusterDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "KubernetesCluster" {
            {
                type: "KubernetesCluster",
                uid:  null,
                name: null,
                unmapped: resource.kubernetesDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "ECSCluster" {
            {
                type: "ECSCluster",
                uid:  resource.ecsClusterDetails.arn,
                name: resource.ecsClusterDetails.name,
                unmapped: resource.ecsClusterDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "Container" {
            {
                type: "Container",
                uid:  resource.containerDetails.id,
                name: resource.containerDetails.name,
                unmapped: resource.containerDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "RDSDBInstance" {
            {
                type: "RDSDBInstance",
                uid:  resource.rdsDbInstanceDetails.dbInstanceArn,
                name: resource.rdsDbInstanceDetails.dbInstanceIdentifier,
                unmapped: resource.rdsDbInstanceDetails
            }
        }
        resource.resourceType == "Lambda" {
            {
                type: "Lambda",
                uid:  resource.lambdaDetails.functionArn,
                name: resource.lambdaDetails.functionName,
                unmapped: resource.lambdaDetails
            }
        }
        default {
            // Unknown resourceType: emit a stub with the raw type
            // string so OCSF consumers see *something* and the
            // operator sees the new resourceType surface in their
            // SIEM. We deliberately do NOT route to error_log here:
            // the finding's classification (the `type` field) is
            // still meaningful even when the resource shape is one
            // we haven't taught the parser. New AWS resourceTypes
            // get added several times a year; failing the whole
            // finding on a resource we can't fully unpack would
            // discard real detections.
            {
                type: resource.resourceType,
                uid:  null,
                name: null,
                unmapped: resource
            }
        }
    }
}

// service.evidence.threatIntelligenceDetails[] surface. GuardDuty
// wraps each TI hit as `{threatListName, threatNames[]}`, which
// does not align with OCSF's Evidence Artifacts object shape
// (OCSF expects a sparse object keyed by artifact type — process,
// file, http_request, etc.). Rather than synthesise a fake shape
// we pass the raw vendor array through under `unmapped.evidence`
// so a consumer that knows GuardDuty can correlate by feed name
// without us mis-mapping the schema.
def function aws_guardduty_threat_intel(service) {
    switch true {
        service == null                                          { null }
        service.evidence == null                                 { null }
        default {
            service.evidence.threatIntelligenceDetails
        }
    }
}

// Build the Detection Finding (2004) record. The dispatcher has
// already validated `type_parts.category` against the closed
// GuardDuty taxonomy by the time we get here, so the per-branch
// processes can share one body without re-checking the category.
def function aws_guardduty_finding_record(body, type_parts, attack_tactic, severity_number) {
    {
        class_uid:    2004,
        category_uid: 2,
        activity_id:  1,                                          // Create (a new finding)
        type_uid:     2004 * 100 + 1,
        severity_number: severity_number,
        status_id:    99,                                         // Other (GuardDuty doesn't carry a remediation status on the finding itself)
        time:         to_int(parse_datetime_rfc3339(body.service.eventLastSeen)),
        start_time:   to_int(parse_datetime_rfc3339(body.service.eventFirstSeen)),
        finding_info: {
            uid:   body.id,
            title: body.title,
            desc:  body.description,
            types: [type_parts.category, type_parts.resource_type, type_parts.threat_name],
            src_url: null
        },
        attacks: [{
            // GuardDuty's ThreatPurpose categories align with MITRE
            // ATT&CK tactic names for the Recon / Discovery /
            // Persistence / PrivilegeEscalation / DefenseEvasion /
            // Execution / Exfiltration / Impact / InitialAccess
            // values; the AWS-specific buckets (UnauthorizedAccess,
            // Backdoor, Trojan, CryptoCurrency, Stealth, PenTest,
            // Policy) don't map cleanly so we pass the raw value
            // through and let the consumer's correlation layer
            // bucket them. The dispatcher above sets `attack_tactic`
            // to the AWS taxonomy name verbatim.
            tactic:    { name: attack_tactic },
            technique: { name: type_parts.threat_name }
        }],
        resources: [aws_guardduty_resource(body.resource)],
        cloud: {
            provider:  "AWS",
            region:    body.region,
            account:   { uid: body.accountId },
            zone:      null
        },
        count:    body.service.count,
        message:  body.description,
        unmapped: {
            arn:           body.arn,
            partition:     body.partition,
            schemaVersion: body.schemaVersion,
            detectorId:    body.service.detectorId,
            archived:      body.service.archived,
            createdAt:     body.createdAt,
            updatedAt:     body.updatedAt,
            severity:      body.severity,
            service_action: body.service.action,
            additionalInfo: body.service.additionalInfo,
            resourceRole:   body.service.resourceRole,
            evidence:       aws_guardduty_threat_intel(body.service)
        },
        metadata: {
            product: {
                vendor_name: "Amazon Web Services",
                name:        "GuardDuty"
            },
            version: "1.3.0"
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main parser
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Parse the JSON, split the `type` field, validate the leading
// ThreatPurpose category against the closed GuardDuty taxonomy,
// then dispatch to a per-category leaf. Every leaf builds the
// same 2004 record — the dispatch exists to enforce the closed
// vocabulary (loud-fail-fast on a category AWS hasn't published
// yet) and to populate the MITRE-aligned `attacks[].tactic.name`.

// Process: parse_aws_guardduty
// Summary: AWS GuardDuty findings JSON → LSIS Detection Finding.
// Reads: ingress
// Writes: workspace.lsis.parsed.*
//         .class_uid (required, Int)
def process parse_aws_guardduty {
    workspace.gd = parse_json(ingress)
    workspace.gd_type = aws_guardduty_split_type(workspace.gd.type)
    switch true {
        workspace.gd_type == null {
            error "parse_aws_guardduty: `type` field does not match GuardDuty `<Category>:<ResourceType>/<ThreatName>` shape: ${workspace.gd.type}"
        }
        default { process parse_aws_guardduty_dispatch }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Category dispatcher
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Each branch is a one-liner that calls aws_guardduty_finding_record
// with the MITRE-ATT&CK tactic name appropriate for the GuardDuty
// ThreatPurpose. The mapping is documented in
// https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_finding-types-active.html
// (the per-finding-type pages enumerate the ThreatPurpose taxonomy).

def process parse_aws_guardduty_finding {
    workspace.gd_severity_number = aws_guardduty_severity_number(workspace.gd.severity)
    switch workspace.gd.severity != null and workspace.gd_severity_number == null {
        true { error "parse_aws_guardduty: invalid GuardDuty severity ${workspace.gd.severity}" }
    }
    workspace.lsis.parsed = aws_guardduty_finding_record(
        workspace.gd,
        workspace.gd_type,
        workspace.gd_tactic,
        workspace.gd_severity_number
    )
}

def process parse_aws_guardduty_dispatch {
    switch workspace.gd_type.category {
        "Recon" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Reconnaissance"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Discovery" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Discovery"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "InitialAccess" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Initial Access"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Execution" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Execution"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Persistence" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Persistence"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "PrivilegeEscalation" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Privilege Escalation"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "DefenseEvasion" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Defense Evasion"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Stealth" {
            // AWS-specific bucket: covers log-tampering / audit-
            // disable findings (CloudTrail logging disabled, S3
            // public-access-block disabled, etc.). Closest ATT&CK
            // tactic is Defense Evasion; pass the AWS label through
            // verbatim so the consumer can disambiguate.
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Stealth"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Exfiltration" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Exfiltration"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Impact" {
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Impact"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "UnauthorizedAccess" {
            // AWS-specific: anomalous auth / API-call patterns
            // (ConsoleLoginSuccess.B, MaliciousIPCaller.Custom,
            // TorIPCaller, etc.). Spans ATT&CK Initial Access and
            // Credential Access; pass the AWS label through.
            workspace.gd_tactic = "UnauthorizedAccess"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Backdoor" {
            // AWS-specific: EC2 instance behaving as part of a C2
            // channel (DNS C2, persistent outbound to known C2 IP).
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Backdoor"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Trojan" {
            // AWS-specific: EC2 instance exhibiting trojan-family
            // network behaviour (DGA domain queries, dropper
            // beacons).
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Trojan"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "CryptoCurrency" {
            // AWS-specific: connections to known mining pools /
            // crypto-jacking activity on EC2 / EKS / ECS.
            workspace.gd_tactic = "CryptoCurrency"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "Policy" {
            // AWS-specific: account / org hygiene posture findings
            // (root user activity, password policy weak, S3 block-
            // public-access disabled). Operational, not adversary-
            // driven — pass the AWS label through.
            workspace.gd_tactic = "Policy"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        "PenTest" {
            // AWS-specific: known pen-test tooling user-agents
            // (Kali, ParrotLinux, Pentoo) hitting the API.
            workspace.gd_tactic = "PenTest"
            process parse_aws_guardduty_finding
        }
        default {
            error "parse_aws_guardduty: unknown ThreatPurpose category `${workspace.gd_type.category}` (full type `${workspace.gd.type}`); GuardDuty closed taxonomy was extended — extend the dispatcher"
        }
    }
}

// Only GuardDuty ThreatPurpose values that have an exact ATT&CK tactic
// counterpart are exposed under the public threat.tactic namespace.
// AWS-specific buckets remain under aws.guardduty.*.
def function aws_guardduty_otlp_tactic_name(category) {
    switch category {
        "Recon"               { "Reconnaissance" }
        "Discovery"           { "Discovery" }
        "InitialAccess"       { "Initial Access" }
        "Execution"           { "Execution" }
        "Persistence"         { "Persistence" }
        "PrivilegeEscalation" { "Privilege Escalation" }
        "DefenseEvasion"      { "Defense Evasion" }
        "Exfiltration"        { "Exfiltration" }
        "Impact"              { "Impact" }
        default               { null }
    }
}

// Process: aws_guardduty_to_otlp
// Summary: Project source facts and canonical scalars into source-owned OTLP fields.
// Reads: workspace.gd.*
//        .accountId (optional, String)
//        workspace.gd_type.*
//        .category (optional, String)
// Writes: workspace.lsis.shed.otlp.*
//         .resource.attributes (optional, Array)
//         .log_record.body (optional, Object)
//         .log_record.attributes (optional, Array)
def process aws_guardduty_to_otlp {
    workspace.lsis.shed.otlp.resource.attributes = concat(
        [{ key: "cloud.provider", value: { string_value: "aws" } }],
        switch workspace.gd.accountId != null {
            true { [{ key: "cloud.account.id", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.accountId } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.region != null {
            true { [{ key: "cloud.region", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.region } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.arn != null {
            true { [{ key: "cloud.resource_id", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.arn } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        [{ key: "service.name", value: { string_value: "AWS GuardDuty" } }],
        [{ key: "telemetry.sdk.name", value: { string_value: "limpid" } }],
        [{ key: "telemetry.sdk.version", value: { string_value: version() } }]
    )

    workspace.lsis.shed.otlp.log_record.body = { string_value: ingress }
    workspace.lsis.shed.otlp.log_record.attributes = concat(
        [{ key: "event.kind", value: { string_value: "alert" } }],
        [{ key: "event.category", value: { array_value: { values: [{ string_value: "intrusion_detection" }] } } }],
        [{ key: "event.type", value: { array_value: { values: [{ string_value: "indicator" }] } } }],
        switch workspace.gd.id != null {
            true { [{ key: "event.id", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.id } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.type != null {
            true { [{ key: "event.code", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.type } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.title != null {
            true { [{ key: "rule.name", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.title } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.description != null {
            true { [{ key: "message", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.description } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch aws_guardduty_otlp_tactic_name(workspace.gd_type.category) != null {
            true { [{ key: "threat.tactic.name", value: { string_value: aws_guardduty_otlp_tactic_name(workspace.gd_type.category) } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd_type.threat_name != null {
            true { [{ key: "aws.guardduty.threat.name", value: { string_value: workspace.gd_type.threat_name } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.service.detectorId != null {
            true { [{ key: "aws.guardduty.detector.id", value: { string_value: workspace.gd.service.detectorId } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd_type.resource_type != null {
            true { [{ key: "aws.guardduty.resource.type", value: { string_value: workspace.gd_type.resource_type } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.service.count != null {
            true { [{ key: "aws.guardduty.finding.count", value: { int_value: to_int(workspace.gd.service.count) } }] }
            default { [] }
        },
        switch workspace.gd.service.archived != null {
            true { [{ key: "aws.guardduty.finding.archived", value: { bool_value: workspace.gd.service.archived } }] }
            default { [] }
        }
    )
}
